# A status `exit` cannot end a program with, refused where it is written
# (task 2250: panel 230's fallback, its condition met by decision 2241;
# design.md §1.12, §4.17; spec § 11).
#
# WHY A STATUS IS 0 TO 255. A shell keeps the low eight bits of a status
# (POSIX's `_exit`), so `exit(code: 256)` once ended a program the shell read
# as a success, and said nothing (defect 2224). The library's `exit` takes an
# `i64`, and where the code is outside 0 to 255 it stops the program at the
# program's own call, as `args()` stops on an argument that is not text: a
# `fail` handed to `.must()` inside the library, whose report names the
# caller's place (`emit/stop_place.hero`), or, through a function value,
# the library's declaration, the one place such a call has (panel 230's R1,
# `emit/signature.hero`'s `value_name`). The failing value is an `i64?`,
# the one option type the library carried before, so a program that never
# calls `exit` keeps its C but for its key of its sources. It never returns, and `hero_exit`
# says so with `_Noreturn`, which keeps clang's flow analysis from reading on
# past it.
#
# WHAT IS REFUSED HERE, AND WHAT IS NOT. A literal written as the code, under
# its minus signs, is refused: its value is in the source, so the program is
# told before it runs, as `int_out_of_range`, the code a literal outside its
# range already carries. Anything else is a computed code and stops the
# program where it runs, an operator on literals included: panel 206's R2 kept
# literal arithmetic in a function body an abort where it runs, since a
# refusal there refuses code that never runs.
#
# AFTER `given_width.after_call`, and the order is load-bearing: that pass
# rewrites the note of each `int_out_of_range` at a literal a parameter of the
# library's measured, saying the width is not the program's to widen, and this
# refusal is not about a width: `256` fits an `i64`.

use ast
use diag
use source
use token
use check/contextual
use check/settling
use check/state

# After a call was checked: where the callee is the library's `exit`, each
# value it is handed (the receiver, where the dot gave one, then the
# arguments) that is a literal outside 0 to 255, unless that literal was
# already told it does not fit, which says more.
function after_call(@c: state.Checker, a: ast.Ast, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], call: settling.Call, given: [ast.Arg])
    if !is_exit(s, decls, decl: call.decl)
        return
    values: [i64] @= []

    if call.receiver >= 0
        values @ values.push(call.receiver)

    for arg in given
        values @ values.push(arg.value)

    for value in values
        if contextual.number_literal(a, value)
            literal = contextual.literal_value(a, s, value)
            span = a.exprs[value].span

            if !literal.is_err() && !a_status(literal.must()) && !told(c, span: span)
                state.push_diagnostic(
                    @c,
                    refused(text: source.span_text(s, span), span: span, low: low_byte(literal.must()))
                )

# The library's `exit`, asked of the declaration as `check/path_end.hero` asks
# it of a call: no program may declare `exit` (`builtin_name_taken`), so the
# name and the library's source are the whole question.
function is_exit(s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], decl: i64) -> bool
    return source.is_library(s, decls[decl].name.start) && source.span_text(s, decls[decl].name) == "exit"

# 0 to 255, minus zero being zero.
function a_status(v: contextual.LitValue) -> bool
    return v.magnitude == 0 || !v.negative && v.magnitude <= 255

# The status a shell would have read: the low eight bits, two's complement
# for a negative code, so `-1` is 255.
function low_byte(v: contextual.LitValue) -> u64
    low = v.magnitude % 256

    if v.negative && low != 0
        return 256 - low
    return low

function told(c: state.Checker, span: token.Span) -> bool
    for d in c.out.diagnostics
        if d.code == "int_out_of_range" && d.span.start == span.start && d.span.end == span.end
            return true

    return false

function refused(text: str, span: token.Span, low: u64) -> diag.Diagnostic
    d: diag.Diagnostic @= diag.new_diagnostic(
        code: "int_out_of_range",
        message: "`" + text + "` is not a status `exit` can end a program with, which is 0 to 255",
        span: span
    )
    reached: str @= "`" + text + "` would reach it as " + low.to_str()

    if low == 0
        reached @ reached + ", the status that says the program succeeded"
    d.notes @ d.notes.push(
        "a shell keeps only the low eight bits of a status, so " + reached + ". Write a number from 0 to 255; a code computed as the program runs is checked at this call, and one outside them stops the program there"
    )
    return d

## Tests

test "a status is 0 to 255, and minus zero is zero"
    assert a_status(contextual.LitValue(negative: false, magnitude: 0))
    assert a_status(contextual.LitValue(negative: false, magnitude: 255))
    assert !a_status(contextual.LitValue(negative: false, magnitude: 256))
    assert a_status(contextual.LitValue(negative: true, magnitude: 0))
    assert !a_status(contextual.LitValue(negative: true, magnitude: 1))

test "the note says what a shell would have read, a negative code by two's complement"
    assert low_byte(contextual.LitValue(negative: false, magnitude: 256)) == 0
    assert low_byte(contextual.LitValue(negative: false, magnitude: 300)) == 44
    assert low_byte(contextual.LitValue(negative: true, magnitude: 1)) == 255
    assert low_byte(contextual.LitValue(negative: true, magnitude: 256)) == 0
    d = refused(text: "256", span: token.Span(start: 4, end: 7), low: 0)
    assert d.code == "int_out_of_range"
    assert d.span.start == 4 && d.span.end == 7
    assert d.message == "`256` is not a status `exit` can end a program with, which is 0 to 255"
    assert d.notes == ["a shell keeps only the low eight bits of a status, so `256` would reach it as 0, the status that says the program succeeded. Write a number from 0 to 255; a code computed as the program runs is checked at this call, and one outside them stops the program there"]
    minus = refused(text: "-1", span: token.Span(start: 0, end: 2), low: 255)
    assert minus.notes[0] == "a shell keeps only the low eight bits of a status, so `-1` would reach it as 255. Write a number from 0 to 255; a code computed as the program runs is checked at this call, and one outside them stops the program there"
